<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">French Cilicia</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1921 CE · capital Adana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French military occupation of Cilicia after the First World War, whose collapse ended the hopes of returning Armenian refugees.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a>, British and then French forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from late 1918, and in 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took over full responsibility, administering it as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/occupied-enemy-territory-administration">Occupied Enemy Territory Administration</a>. Tens of thousands of Armenian survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> returned with French support.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> governed through military administrators such as Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edouard-bremond">Edouard Bremond</a>, relying partly on the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>. The administration encouraged Armenian resettlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Marash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a>, which alarmed Turkish Muslims. Nationalist resistance grew, and in January-February 1920 fighting in Marash ended with a French withdrawal and the deaths of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. Sieges followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a> and Aintab.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ankara-1921">Treaty of Ankara</a> in October 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised the nationalist <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a> and withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> by early 1922. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> left with the French, many settling in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1918 CE – 1921 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.87%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1921 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edouard Bremond</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Turkish, Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>OETA North, Occupied Enemy Territory Administration North</dd></dl></aside></div>

